WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.751
Before choosing furniture, Cedar Crossing listens and
observes.

2
00:00:03.951 --> 00:00:10.669
Staff ask current users, reach out to people who rarely
visit, and record when people cannot find suitable
space.

3
00:00:10.869 --> 00:00:13.370
They separate what they know from what they assume.

4
00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:16.332
The visual uses SWOT to organize evidence.

5
00:00:16.532 --> 00:00:24.039
Strengths and weaknesses are internal to the library:
movable tables are a strength; limited staff capacity is
a weakness.

6
00:00:24.239 --> 00:00:33.506
Opportunities and threats come from outside: a possible
community partnership is an opportunity; a proposed
reduction in municipal funding is a threat.

7
00:00:33.706 --> 00:00:38.118
These are hypothetical conditions, not findings about a
real library.

8
00:00:38.318 --> 00:00:45.136
PEST looks specifically at political, economic, social,
and technological forces outside the organization.

9
00:00:45.336 --> 00:00:49.855
It can help identify the external conditions that enter
a SWOT analysis.

10
00:00:50.055 --> 00:00:52.942
The two tools serve related but different purposes.

11
00:00:53.142 --> 00:00:55.335
What matters is the action that follows.

12
00:00:55.535 --> 00:01:01.410
Limited staff capacity might rule out a labor-intensive
option or require a smaller pilot.

13
00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.259
A possible partnership needs verification.

14
00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:10.902
An attractive list of possibilities cannot substitute
for evidence about needs, constraints, and feasibility.

